{#-
  BFFO layout: the default, with the website's header and footer.

  It extends nothing -- minijinja resolves by file name, so this file
  *replaces* the embedded base -- which means the accessibility floor in the
  original's header comment is this file's responsibility now. Kept verbatim
  and deliberately:

  * `charset` first in head, `viewport` second;
  * the skip link first in body, outside every landmark, targeting
    `main[tabindex="-1"]`;
  * the contents navigation before `main` in document order;
  * every piece of content inside `header`, `nav`, `main` or `footer`.

  The two `theme-switch` includes are the same: replacing `base` means
  inheriting nothing from the layout it replaced, so a theme that wants the
  colour-scheme control has to say so. They resolve to the embedded partials
  unless this theme ships its own.

  The nav mirrors `bffo-webapp/src/lib/components/Header.svelte`. Its links
  are absolute to bffo.org because the site is a different application on the
  same origin: this documentation is served at /ontology/ and /vocabulary/,
  and Browse, Categories, Domains, Organizations and About all belong to the
  SvelteKit app, which this build does not contain.

  The wordmark is absolute too, and for the same reason as the rest: it means
  BFFO the project, which is the website. That it currently *also* resolves
  through `../` is an accident of this deployment -- the documentation's base
  URL is the origin root, so the two roots coincide -- and an accident is a
  poor thing to navigate by. Publish the documentation at `/docs/` one day
  and the relative form would quietly start landing on the documentation
  index instead.

  `html.absolute-nav-link` would otherwise object, because this build writes
  an `index.html` at that address even though production serves the website
  there. `site.external_paths` in `iyo.toml` says the root is the website's;
  that is a fact about the deployment, and the build cannot infer it.

  No search box. The website's is a client-side index over formats; this tree
  has `terms.json` and no JavaScript, and a box that looked the same and did
  nothing would be worse than its absence.
-#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{ site.lang }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
{%- if site.theme_switch %}{% include "partials/theme-switch.html.jinja" %}{% endif %}
<title>{% block title %}{{ site.title }}{% endblock %}</title>
{%- block meta %}{% endblock %}
<meta name="generator" content="{{ site.generator }} {{ site.generator_version }}">
{%- block links %}{% endblock %}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ page.assets | url }}tokens.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ page.assets | url }}theme.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ page.assets | url }}bffo.css">
{%- block structured_data %}{% endblock %}
</head>
<body>
<a class="skip-link" href="#iyo-main">Skip to main content</a>
<header class="bffo-header">
  <div class="bffo-bar">
    <a class="bffo-wordmark" href="https://bffo.org/">
      <svg class="bffo-mark" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <ellipse cx="12" cy="5" rx="9" ry="3"/>
        <path d="M3 5v14c0 1.66 4.03 3 9 3s9-1.34 9-3V5"/>
        <path d="M3 12c0 1.66 4.03 3 9 3s9-1.34 9-3"/>
      </svg>
      <span>BFFO</span>
    </a>
    <nav class="bffo-nav" aria-label="BFFO site">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://bffo.org/formats/">Browse</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://bffo.org/categories/">Categories</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://bffo.org/domains/">Domains</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://bffo.org/organizations/">Organizations</a></li>
        <li><a href="{{ site.base_url | rel }}" aria-current="true">Ontology</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://bffo.org/about/">About</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
{#-
  The rail and the content, side by side above 60rem and stacked below it.
  Source order is rail then `main`, which is also the reading order a screen
  reader and a keyboard get; the columns are placed by grid, so the visual
  arrangement costs the document order nothing.
-#}
<div class="bffo-layout">
  <div class="bffo-rail">
    <nav aria-label="Vocabularies" class="bffo-rail-nav">
      <p class="nav-title">Vocabularies</p>
      <ul class="nav-list">
        {%- for ns in site.namespaces %}
        <li>
          <a href="{{ ns.url | rel }}"{% if page.collection_url and ns.url == page.collection_url %} aria-current="true"{% endif %}>{{ ns.title | default(ns.iri) }}</a>
          <span class="bffo-count">{{ ns.term_count }}</span>
        </li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </nav>
    {%- block contents %}{% endblock %}
    {%- if site.theme_switch %}{% include "partials/theme-switch-button.html.jinja" %}{% endif %}
  </div>
  <main id="iyo-main" tabindex="-1">
{#-
  The breadcrumb sits inside `main` and before the `h1`. That is allowed and
  is why it is a `nav` with an `ol` rather than anything heading-shaped: the
  accessibility floor forbids a heading before the `h1`, not a landmark.

  The middle crumb is found by matching `page.collection_url` -- the
  namespace document a term belongs to -- against `site.namespaces`, which is
  the only place a namespace's *title* lives. A term page carries the URL and
  not the name, so the theme joins them rather than the tool growing a field.
-#}
    {%- if page.kind == "term" or page.kind == "document" %}
    <nav class="bffo-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="{{ site.base_url | rel }}">{{ site.title }}</a></li>
        {%- if page.kind == "term" %}
          {%- for ns in site.namespaces if ns.url == page.collection_url %}
          <li><a href="{{ ns.url | rel }}">{{ ns.title | default(ns.iri) }}</a></li>
          {%- endfor %}
          <li><span aria-current="page">{{ term.label }}</span></li>
        {%- else %}
          <li><span aria-current="page">{{ document.title }}</span></li>
        {%- endif %}
      </ol>
    </nav>
    {%- endif %}
{% block main %}{% endblock %}
  </main>
</div>
<footer class="bffo-footer">
  <div class="bffo-footer-grid">
    <div>
      <h2>About BFFO</h2>
      <p>The Bio File Formats &amp; Ontology (BFFO) is a structured knowledge base
      documenting file formats used across bioinformatics, genomics, proteomics,
      imaging, and related life sciences.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Quick Links</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://bffo.org/formats/">Browse Formats</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://bffo.org/search/">Search</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://bffo.org/organizations/">Organizations</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://bffo.org/about/">About</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>This documentation</h2>
      <p>
        {%- if page.license %}Vocabulary under <a href="{{ page.license | rel }}">{{ page.license_label }}</a>{% endif %}
        {%- if page.license and site.doc_license %}; documentation under <a href="{{ site.doc_license | rel }}">{{ site.doc_license_label }}</a>{% endif %}
        {%- if not page.license and site.doc_license %}Documentation under <a href="{{ site.doc_license | rel }}">{{ site.doc_license_label }}</a>{% endif %}.
      </p>
      <p class="muted">Generated by <a href="https://github.com/nishad/iyo">{{ site.generator }}</a> {{ site.generator_version }}.
      Machine-readable index: <a href="{{ site.llms_url | rel }}">llms.txt</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="bffo-colophon">BFFO &mdash; Bio File Formats &amp; Ontology</p>
</footer>
</body>
</html>
